In article <40af7a$dqi@news.iii.net>, 
jwright@creepos.iii.net (Joel Wright) writes...

>	I'm looking for jumper info for the DEC RZ25 SCSI disk.

    Here's what I found on a four-year-old spec -- I didn't check it for
    accuracy.

    Jumper options:

    	,-------------------------.
    	|                  [-J7--]|
    	|                   PPPPP |
    	|                   54321 |
    	|[-]P1                    |
    	|[-]P2                    |
    	|[-]P3                    |
    	|[J]P4                    |
    	|[6]P5                    |
    	|[-]P6                    |
    	|[-]P7                    |
    	|[-]P8                    |
    	|                         |
    	|                    P3[J]|
    	|                    P2[5]|
    	|                    P1[-]|
    	|                         |
    	`-------------------------'
    	 """""""""""""""""""  ''''
    	   SCSI connector      DC


    There are four customer usable jumpers available for configuration. 
    Three jumpers on block J5 are used to select the drive's SCSI ID.

    Thre will be a jumper on block J6-P2 to control the spin up mode. 
    THe status of this jumper will be returned by MODE SENSE page 25h.


    JUMPER BLOCK J5 or J7

    	P1	P2	P2	Device
    	out	out	out	0
    	out	out	in	1
    	out	in 	out	2
    	out	in 	in 	3
    	in 	out	out	4
    	in 	out	in 	5
    	in 	in 	out	6
    	in 	in      in 	7

    	
    JUMPER BLOCK J7

    Jumper P4 is for the spindle sync option.  If spindle sync is not used
    a jumper must be installed.

    Jumper P5 is for a remote LED indicator.  The pin closest to the PCB is
    the anode connection to +5 volts through a pullup resistor.  The top
    pin is to the LED signal control.


    JUMPER BLOCK J6

    	pos	use
    	P1	self test for factory use only
    	P2	spin up on power application
    	P3	not for use
    	P4	write protect
    	P5	parity checking enabled
    	P6	reserved for later use
    	P7	drive supplies power to the SCSI bus
    	P8	power to drive internal terminators


    Hope this helps,

    -- Kenny

