peng@pirates.cs.swt.edu (Wuxu Peng) writes:


>Is it possible for normal users to run DCE applications?
>I taught a distributed system class in the spring and DCE
>was part of the topics.  However,  my students could not
>run their DCE programs.  I was told by our Computer Center
>that DEC only allows root to run applications.  Can somebody
>confirm this?  I have to teach the distributed system class
>again in next spring.

In general, ordinary users can run both the client and server sides
of DCE applications.  DCE has no prohibitions against this.
It is possible that your computing center has some policy
about this, or that the person didn't know what they were talking
about.

If the application server does the standard operations of advertising
with CDS and registering security with SEC, there are several
permissions that must be set up correctly before users could start
the server: a directory to advertise in, a principal to authenticate as,
access to a keytab file.

You'll probably either need to enlist the help of someone from
your computing center to set these up, or have your students
run unauthenticated applications and use string binding.

